.TH RETROSAVER 1 "2026-09-30" "retrosaver 0.2.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
retrosaver \- retro screensavers for GNOME on Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B retrosaver
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B retrosaver
brings back late\-1990s XScreenSaver display modules \(em fractals, the
.B atlantis
fish tank, flying toasters, spinning pipes \(em on GNOME desktops running Wayland, where
the traditional screensaver daemon no longer functions.
.PP
It does not fork, patch, or vendor XScreenSaver. It reuses the display modules that Debian
and Ubuntu already package, and supplies only the piece GNOME dropped: an idle trigger and
a fullscreen wrapper. All the artwork belongs to XScreenSaver.
.PP
Idle is detected through
.BR org.gnome.Mutter.IdleMonitor ,
the only idle signal GNOME exposes, since its compositor implements no Wayland idle
protocol.
.SS Stages
Three stages, all timed from when the session goes idle. Any user activity at any stage
tears everything down and re\-arms from zero.
.TP
.B Saver
5 minutes idle by default. Launch a random display module fullscreen, always\-on\-top,
with the pointer hidden. With more than one monitor, each monitor runs its own copy of
the module, sized to that monitor. Every 5 minutes thereafter the module is replaced by another
one, chosen at random from those not yet shown this idle period. Switching modules is
not a stage: it does not advance the sequence, and it stops at the lock threshold. See
.BR CYCLE_AFTER .
Skipped entirely if the session is already locked: GNOME's lock screen sits above every
XWayland window, so a module started behind it would be invisible while still driving the
GPU and keeping the display awake. The lock and blank stages still run.
.TP
.B Lock
20 minutes idle by default. Kill the module and hand off to GNOME's own lock screen via
.BR "loginctl lock\-session" .
.TP
.B Blank
22 minutes idle by default. Power the display off by setting
.B idle\-delay
to 10 seconds, already less than the time the session has been idle, so gnome\-shell
blanks on its next tick. The daemon never pokes DPMS behind the compositor's back.
On user activity
.B idle\-delay
goes back to
.BR 0 .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Runtime
.TP
.B daemon
Run the idle state machine. Normally started by the systemd user unit, not by hand.
Re-reads its configuration on
.B SIGHUP
and whenever the config file changes. See
.B SIGNALS
below.
.TP
.BI run " [module]"
Launch one module fullscreen. Picks at random when
.I module
is omitted. Refuses to stack a second module over a running one. A module named
explicitly is run even if
.B EXCLUDE
lists it.
.TP
.BR stop " [" \-\-keep\-idle\-delay ]
Tear down a running module and clear the runtime state. Safe at any time, and a clean
no\-op when nothing is running. Then restore the
.B idle\-delay
saved at
.BR setup ,
unless a
.B retrosaver daemon
process is still running: the daemon owns
.B idle\-delay
while it runs, so handing it back to GNOME would let gnome\-shell blank the screen out
from under the screensaver. The unit's
.B ExecStopPost
runs after the daemon has exited, so it still restores. If the process list cannot be
read, the value is restored.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-idle\-delay
Never restore
.BR idle\-delay ,
even when no daemon is running.
.RE
.TP
.B list
Print the discovered, selectable modules, one per line.
.SS Installation
.TP
.B setup
Run the preflight checks, write a config if none exists (an existing one is never
overwritten), enable the systemd user unit, and take ownership of
.BR idle\-delay ,
saving the original value first. The preflight refuses to continue unless:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B XDG_SESSION_TYPE
is
.B wayland
and
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
contains
.BR GNOME ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B DISPLAY
is set, so XWayland is reachable;
.IP \(bu 2
.B org.gnome.Mutter.IdleMonitor
answers on the session bus;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR wmctrl ,
.B xdotool
and
.B unclutter\-xfixes
(or
.BR unclutter )
are on
.BR PATH ;
.IP \(bu 2
the configuration parses and leaves at least one module to pick;
.IP \(bu 2
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/retrosaver.service
exists. A source build has no unit to enable, so
.B setup
needs the installed package.
.RE
.TP
.B teardown
Reverse
.BR setup :
disable and stop the unit, stop any running module, restore the saved
.BR idle\-delay ,
and delete the saved copy. The configuration file is kept.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR version ", " \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.PP
With no command, or an unknown one,
.B retrosaver
prints the usage summary to standard error and exits non\-zero.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B retrosaver
reads
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf ,
which is
.I ~/.config/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf
unless
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set.
The file is shell\-shaped
.IR KEY=value ,
but it is
.B parsed, never sourced
\(em it lives in
.I $HOME
and is never executed. A commented example is installed at
.IR /usr/share/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf.example .
.TP
.B SAVER_DELAY
Seconds of idle before the screensaver starts. Default 300.
.TP
.B CYCLE_AFTER
Seconds each module stays on screen before the saver switches to another one. Default
300.
.B 0
disables switching, leaving the module that started the saver stage up until the lock.
Modules already shown this idle period are not repeated; once every selectable module
has been shown the set starts over. If only one module is selectable, nothing switches.
.TP
.B LOCK_AFTER
Seconds after the screensaver starts before the session locks. Default 900.
.B 0
disables locking.
.TP
.B BLANK_AFTER
Seconds after locking before the display powers off. Default 120.
.B 0
disables blanking, as does
.B LOCK_AFTER=0
\(em blanking without locking is meaningless.
.TP
.B EXCLUDE
Space\-separated module names never to pick. The default excludes modules that need image
assets, network access, or elevated capabilities and misbehave standalone.
.TP
.B INCLUDE
Optional. If non\-empty, pick only from this space\-separated list.
.PP
Durations are whole, non\-negative seconds. Unknown keys are ignored.
.PP
Changes take effect as soon as the file is saved: the daemon watches it with inotify and
re-arms itself.
.B "systemctl \-\-user reload retrosaver"
does the same on demand. When the settings have actually changed, a reload behaves like
user activity \(em any module on screen is torn down and the stages re-arm from zero.
Saving the file without changing a setting does nothing. A configuration that fails to
parse is
.B not
applied: the error is logged and the daemon keeps the settings it already had, because
exiting would leave the session with no screensaver and no auto\-lock. If the file is
already broken when the daemon starts, it runs on the built\-in defaults;
.BR run ,
.B list
and
.B setup
refuse to run until it is fixed.
.PP
Modules are discovered by intersecting the basenames of
.I /usr/share/xscreensaver/config/*.xml
with the executables in
.IR /usr/libexec/xscreensaver/ ,
so helper binaries are never selected and the list follows the installed packages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf
User configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/retrosaver/idle\-delay.orig
The
.B idle\-delay
value saved at
.BR setup ,
restored by
.BR teardown .
.TP
.I /usr/share/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf.example
Commented example configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/retrosaver.service
The systemd user unit. Shipped by the package but deliberately not enabled;
.B retrosaver setup
enables it, because enabling a
.B systemd \-\-user
unit needs the user's own session bus.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/retrosaver.pid
PIDs of the running module, one per line: one copy per monitor, primary first. Every PID read back is checked against
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
before being signalled, because the file can be stale after a crash and PIDs are recycled.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/retrosaver.module
Name of the running module.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/retrosaver.unclutter.pid
PID of the pointer\-hiding
.B unclutter\-xfixes
process started with the module.
.PP
A module removes these runtime files only while they still name it, so switching
modules never erases the replacement's state.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RETROSAVER_LOG_LEVEL
The daemon's log level:
.BR debug ,
.BR info
(the default),
.BR warn
or
.BR error .
An unreadable value is logged and ignored. Set it in the unit with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user edit retrosaver" ,
adding
.B Environment=RETROSAVER_LOG_LEVEL=debug
under
.BR [Service] .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration and the saved
.B idle\-delay
live. Defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the runtime state files live. Defaults to
.IR /run/user/<uid> .
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_ID
The login session to lock, and whose lock state is checked before the saver stage
starts. When unset, the user's display session as logind reports it.
.TP
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE ", " XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ", " DISPLAY
Checked by the
.B setup
preflight, and
.B DISPLAY
is how display modules reach XWayland.
.SH CAVEATS
.SS This is not a screen locker
A display module is an ordinary X11 window and cannot secure the session; X11 grabs do not
work under XWayland. Real security comes from the lock stage handing off to GNOME's own
lock screen. Anyone wanting the screensaver itself to lock is out of luck on Wayland, by
design.
.SS The daemon owns the idle policy
.B setup
sets
.B org.gnome.desktop.session idle\-delay
to
.BR 0 .
That is what stops gnome\-shell blanking the screen out from under the screensaver, and it
also disables GNOME's idle\-dim.
.B "Consequently, if the daemon is not running there is no auto\-lock at all."
The unit uses
.B Restart=always
and
.B ExecStopPost
to restore
.B idle\-delay
on any exit, and
.B teardown
restores it permanently.
.SS Other limitations
Modules that grab and distort a desktop screenshot render a solid colour, because
.B grim
does not work under GNOME's compositor. GL modules run through XWayland and keep the GPU
awake. On a laptop,
.B sleep\-inactive\-battery\-timeout
may suspend the machine before the lock and blank stages land.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration file and re-arm, without restarting. The process, its D-Bus
connection and its ownership of
.B idle\-delay
are all preserved, where a restart drops them for a moment. The systemd unit exposes this
as
.BR "systemctl \-\-user reload retrosaver" .
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Tear down cleanly, stopping any running module and restoring
.BR idle\-delay .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every state transition logs to standard error, so
.B journalctl \-\-user \-u retrosaver
reads as a clear trace.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR gsettings (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
and the display modules' own pages in section 6x, such as
.BR atlantis (6x).
.PP
Project home: https://github.com/c-premus/retrosaver
.SH AUTHOR
Chris <63758245+c-premus@users.noreply.github.com>.
.PP
The display modules themselves are XScreenSaver, by Jamie Zawinski and contributors.
.B retrosaver
is only the idle trigger and the fullscreen wrapper around them.
